\title[The complete $p$-biased Fourier expansion and exact influence defects]
      {The Complete $p$-Biased Fourier Expansion on the Discrete Cube\\
       and Exact Defects in the Influence Inequalities}

\author{Aristotle}
\date{7 August 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We develop, from first principles and by purely finite-algebraic means, the complete
$p$-biased Fourier analysis of real-valued functions on the discrete cube
$\{0,1\}^{\iota}$ over a finite index set $\iota$, and use it to convert two classical
influence inequalities for monotone events into exact identities.

The development rests on a single engine, the \emph{product rule} for the biased
expectation: the expectation of a product of one-coordinate functions factorizes.
From it we obtain full orthogonality of the biased Walsh characters
$\psi_S=\prod_{v\in S}\psi_v$, namely $\E_p[\psi_S\psi_T]=\ind[S=T](pq)^{|S|}$ with
$q=1-p$; a reproducing-kernel identity exhibiting
$\sum_S\prod_{v\in S}\psi_v(\xi)\psi_v(\eta)/(pq)$ as a reweighted Kronecker delta;
the resulting \emph{completeness} $f=\sum_S\hat f(S)\psi_S$ for $0<p<1$; and
\emph{Parseval's identity} $\E_p[fg]=\sum_S(pq)^{|S|}\hat f(S)\hat g(S)$ with the
variance specialization $\Var_p(f)=\sum_{S\neq\emptyset}(pq)^{|S|}\hat f(S)^2$.

Two applications follow. For an increasing event $A$ with $P=\PP_p(A)$ and influences
$I_v$, the $\pm1$-indicator satisfies the exact energy decomposition
$4P(1-P)=4pq\sum_vI_v^2+\sum_{|S|\ge2}E_S$ with $E_S=(pq)^{|S|}\hat g(S)^2\ge0$,
exhibiting the $\ell^2$ influence bound as the assertion that the level-$\ge2$
energy is nonnegative. Via the one-coordinate decomposition $f=A_vf+\psi_vD_vf$ we
prove the biased site-energy identity $\sum_{S\ni v}E_S(f)=pq\,\E_p[(D_vf)^2]$ and
hence the exact Efron--Stein/Poincar\'e defect
$pq\sum_v\E_p[(D_vf)^2]-\Var_p(f)=\sum_{S\neq\emptyset}(|S|-1)E_S(f)\ge0$ for
arbitrary real functions, together with its specialization to increasing events and
the characterization of equality. Consequently the two classical influence
inequalities, historically proved by unrelated arguments and pointing in opposite
directions, are two differently-weighted readings of one and the same level-energy
ledger, and are tight under the identical condition.
\end{abstract}

